{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a French nobleman who was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was a member of the Anjou dynasty and was known for his military campaigns in the Middle East.\n\n**Early Life and Reign** \n\nRaymond III was the second son of William II the Good Count of Tripoli and his wife Adela of Bourgogne. He became Count of Tripoli in 1152 after the death of his older brother. Raymond III was a skilled military leader and expanded the territory of Tripoli through his campaigns. He also strengthened the city's defenses and built several castles.\n\n**Military Campaigns** \n\nRaymond III was a successful military leader who expanded the territory of Tripoli through several campaigns. He conquered the surrounding cities and towns and established a strong foothold in the region. He also fought against the Fatimid Caliphate and the Ayyubid dynasty. Raymond III's military campaigns were marked by their brutality and efficiency. He was known for his use of sieges and his ability to use the terrain to his advantage.\n\n* **The Crusader States** \n\nRaymond III was a member of the Crusader States and played an important role in the First Crusade. He was a key ally of the Normans and worked closely with them to defend their territories. He also fought against the Fatimid Caliphate and the Ayyubid dynasty. Raymond III's military campaigns in the Middle East had a significant impact on the region and helped to shape its history.\n\n**Personal Life** \n\nRaymond III was married to Eschiva of Alpilles and had several children with her. He was also known to have had a mistress named Maria de Albarracín. Raymond III was a devout Christian and was known for his piety. He was also a skilled diplomat and was able to maintain good relations with the Muslim leaders of the region.\n\n**Death and Legacy** \n\nRaymond III died in 1187 during the Third Crusade. He was killed in the Battle of Hattin while fighting against the Ayyubid dynasty. Raymond III's legacy is complex and multifaceted. He was a skilled military leader who expanded the territory of Tripoli and played an important role in the Crusader States. He was also a devout Christian who worked to maintain good relations with the Muslim leaders of the region.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0016098171472549438}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0001308832288486883}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.16895784437656403}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.802703940891661e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.0002967870968859643}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -7.068861305015162e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008241336792707443}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.3622196912765503}, {"token": " French", "logprob": -4.904288291931152}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.04395672306418419}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.004651796538382769}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.9146634340286255}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -2.658663272857666}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.15602856874465942}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.6203789114952087}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.3841574147809297e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.0013148480793461204}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -2.0503786799963564e-05}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.030820207670331}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.039031412452459335}, {"token": "115", "logprob": -0.6273841857910156}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.003271230263635516}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.11336828023195267}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.002859553089365363}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -0.00043394684325903654}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.006069205701351166}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.5139465176616795e-05}, {"token": "118", "logprob": -0.4793263077735901}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -0.002806655131280422}, {"token": ".", "lo